]\nThis is a linear function and does not have any singularities.\n\nTo find the sum of all complex solutions, we need to consider the symmetry and the behavior of the equation. Notice that if \\(x\\) is a solution, then \\(-x\\) is also a solution because the left-hand side is an even function (it does not change if we replace \\(x\\) with \\(-x\\)). Therefore, the solutions come in pairs \\((x, -x)\\).\n\nThe sum of each pair \\((x, -x)\\) is zero. Since the solutions come in pairs and there are no other specific solutions that would break this symmetry, the sum of all solutions is zero.\n\nThus, the sum of all complex solutions of the equation is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:123"}
